[Event "Ruy Lopez for White"]
[Lesson "exchange-idea"]
[LessonTitle "The Exchange Variation"]
[Step "explain"]
[TeacherNotes "White gives the bishop for the knight and doubles Black's c-pawns | Black gets two bishops, but White gets a healthier pawn structure | Later, White often plays d4 to trade pawns and head for a simple, clear game | Names from the openings list: Ruy Lopez: Exchange Variation, Normal Variation"]
[Mistakes "Grabbing the e5 pawn at once with the knight | Forgetting to castle | Thinking a trade of bishop for knight loses material. It is an even trade"]
[ClassQuestions "Why are two pawns on the c-file a weakness? | Who has the two bishops after the trade? | Why is a simple plan good for White here?"]

{The idea: trade the bishop for the knight. Black's pawns become doubled on the c-file. [%cal Gb5c6,Rd7c6] [%csl Rc7,Rc6]}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 {The bishop takes the knight. [%cal Gb5c6]} 4... dxc6 {Black retakes with the d-pawn. Now Black has two pawns on the c-file.} 5. O-O {White castles and keeps a calm, healthy position. [%cal Ge1g1]} *

[Event "Ruy Lopez for White"]
[Lesson "exchange-idea"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/1ppp1ppp/p1n5/1B2p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 4"]
[Hint "Use your bishop."]
[Hint "Capture the knight on c6."]

{White to move. Play the Exchange Variation: take the knight on c6 with your bishop.}
4. Bxc6 {Black will have doubled pawns on the c-file. [%cal Gb5c6]} *

[Event "Ruy Lopez for White"]
[Lesson "exchange-idea"]
[Step "line-drill"]
[Side "white"]
[Hint "Develop the knight, then the bishop to b5."]
[Hint "When Black plays ...a6, take the knight on c6."]
[Hint "Finish by castling."]

{Play the Exchange Variation as White.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. O-O *

[Event "Ruy Lopez for White"]
[Lesson "exchange-structure"]
[LessonTitle "Plans and pawn structure"]
[Step "explain"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/1pp2ppp/p1p5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQ1RK1 b kq - 1 5"]
[TeacherNotes "Black's doubled c-pawns are a long-term weakness | Black has two bishops, so open lines are Black's friend; White tries not to give Black easy play | White's plan: put pieces on good squares, then play d4 to open the center | After d4 and a pawn trade, White has more pawns on the kingside than Black. That can become a winning edge in an endgame | Plan for Black: use the bishops and aim for quick activity"]
[Mistakes "Forgetting that the c-pawn weakness only matters in a long game | Opening the center before the pieces are ready | Giving Black easy, quick play"]
[ClassQuestions "Why does White want the game to last a long time? | Which pawn push opens the center for White? | Why do two bishops like open lines?"]

{After 5.O-O, White has a better pawn shape. Black has doubled pawns on c7 and c6. White plans d4 to open the center. [%cal Gd2d4] [%csl Rc7,Rc6]}
*

[Event "Ruy Lopez for White"]
[Lesson "exchange-structure"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/1pp2ppp/p1p5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[Hint "Use your king."]
[Hint "Castle short, so the king goes to g1."]

{White to move. Do not rush. Make your king safe before anything else.}
5. O-O {Safe first. [%cal Ge1g1]} *

[Event "Ruy Lopez for White"]
[Lesson "exchange-grab"]
[LessonTitle "Do not grab on e5"]
[Step "explain"]
[TeacherNotes "A pawn grab can look free but may not be | The black queen fork on d4 hits the knight on e5 and the pawn on e4 | White ends up level on pawns, with the king in the middle | A calm 5.O-O avoids all this"]
[Mistakes "Taking a pawn before castling | Ignoring the queen's fork | Believing that a capture is always a gain"]
[ClassQuestions "Which two things does Qd4 attack? | Why is the white king in check at the end? | What is the calm move instead?"]

{A tempting move: take the e5 pawn. But watch what the black queen does. [%cal Gf3e5]}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. Nxe5 {White grabs a pawn.} 5... Qd4 {The queen attacks the knight on e5 and the pawn on e4. [%cal Rd4e5,Rd4e4]} 6. Nf3 {The knight goes back.} 6... Qxe4+ {Black takes the e4 pawn with check. The pawns are level again and the white king is in check. [%cal Re4e1]} *

[Event "Ruy Lopez for White"]
[Lesson "exchange-grab"]
[Step "explain"]

{White took e5 but Black took e4. Both sides have seven pawns. So 5.Nxe5 did not win a pawn for free. The calm 5.O-O is better.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. Nxe5 Qd4 6. Nf3 Qxe4+ *
